.TH CHMAP_INIT_MP 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_init_mp \- create a hash map with a custom allocator into an already declared handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_init_mp(hm_name, mmgmt_procs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a hash map that takes all of its memory from mmgmt_procs, and
assigns it to hm_name. The caller must declare the handle first, with
chmap_declare(3) or chmap_declare_scoped(3). The key and value types are
not arguments here: the macro reads them from the companion type variables
that the declare macro created.

This macro is chmap_init(3) plus an allocator, and the map uses its own
hashing for the declared key type. chmap_init_ch(3) replaces the hashing
instead, and chmap_init_full(3) takes both.

The one-step equivalents are chmap_construct_mp(3) and
chmap_construct_mp_scoped(3).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name      The already declared map handle to create the map into
mmgmt_procs  Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, or NULL for the default
             allocator
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro expands to a statement and does not expand to an
expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails
All four members of ccol_memmgmt_procs_t must be non-NULL
The macro copies the procedures into the map, so the caller's own
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t need not outlive the call; the procedures
themselves must stay callable for the whole life of the map
Everything that the map allocates later, its bucket array included,
comes from these procedures, and everything that the map frees goes
back to them
Uses CCOL_DEFAULT_INITIAL_BUCKET_ARRAY_SIZE (16) buckets
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};

chmap_declare(scores, char *, int);
chmap_init_mp(scores, &procs);
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(scores, "alice", score);
chmap_destroy(scores);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_init (3),
.BR chmap_init_ch (3),
.BR chmap_init_full (3),
.BR chmap_declare (3),
.BR chmap_construct_mp (3),
.BR chmap_create_full (3),
.BR chmap_destroy (3)
